## Frontier drought — keep the agent, swap the model

Canonical pins above stay. Do **not** rewrite `.cursor/agents/` frontmatter for a dry
pool. The next parent that follows this rule would just put Sol/Opus back.

**Drought is ON** when any of these is true in this conversation:

- Matthew says frontier credits are out / dry / empty
- A Task (or `@` spawn) fails with `Other Models usage limit reached`
- Cursor reports it switched off a frontier model because that pool is empty

Once ON, it stays ON for the rest of the conversation. Do not probe Sol or Opus
again to "check". **Drought is OFF** when Matthew says credits are back, or a
frontier spawn actually succeeds.

**While drought is ON:**

| Lane | Still dispatch | Task `model` |
|---|---|---|
| Think + challenge + structured verification (Clive, Clive's Man, Pam, Doc triage, Ruth Hadley, Lazlo, Kathryn, Workshop Proposer/Challenger, Ruth challengers) | The **named** agent | `cursor-grok-4.6-xhigh-fast` |
| Build | Named builder (Kate, Vercel, Brain-base) | `cursor-grok-4.5-high-fast` or inherit — already first-party |
| Grind / hands (executors, Workshop builders, mechanical edits) | The **named** minion | `composer-2.5-fast` |

- **Never skip the lane.** Empty credits are not a reason to answer as Clive, Pam, or
  Doc yourself. Same agent, cheaper model.
- **Never pass** `gpt-5.6-sol-xhigh`, `claude-opus-5-thinking-high`,
  `claude-sonnet-5-thinking-xhigh`, or Kimi in the Task `model` field while dry.
  That is what empties the spawn.
- If a spawn fails on the usage-limit error, **retry once immediately** with the
  same `subagent_type` and brief, `model` set to the drought row above. Then tell
  Matthew in one line that the head is running on Grok until credits refill.
- If the catalog rejects `cursor-grok-4.6-xhigh-fast`, use `inherit` when the
  parent is already Grok. Do not invent a third slug.

This is a spend fallback, not a new doctrine. Independent judgement (OpenAI vs
Anthropic) waits until the frontier pool has money again.

## Dispatch gate — check the roster first

**This is a precondition, not advice.** Before launching any generic worker (explore, generalPurpose, shell, or a plain Composer subagent), check whether a named AstraJax agent owns that lane. If one does, invoke that agent instead. Generic exploration is the fallback, never the opening move.

| Work | Named agent |
|-----|-----|
| Repo truth, context, architecture, "how does this fit together?" | **@clive** |
| Sceptical pass: assumptions, evidence, pre-mortem, decision gate | **@pam** |
| Context engine — intake, curation, publish prep | **@clive-man** |
| Reading Hyperagent release emails into the repo | **@clive-hyperagent-release-scanner** |
| Build work when you're unsure which builder | **@doc** (triage + dispatch) |
| Front-end UI and interface work — "make this interface work / look right", including the painted-world rooms, scenes and hotspots. Works closely with **@kathryn-goodchild** on visual direction | **@kate** |
| Wiring to data, API routes, env, build and deploy — "get this working and shipped" | **@doc-vercel-minion** |
| Airtable base scaffolding for brains | **@doc-brain-base-builder** |
| Creating or changing agents themselves | **@doc-workshop-proposer** |
| Updating a live HyperAgent skill body/scripts | **Skill Forge** via HyperAgent MCP (`create_thread` + attached skill JSON) — not Doc |
| Airtable data layer — schema, doctrine, control plane | **@ruth-hadley** |
| Character spine, voice, dramaturgy | **@lazlo-marlowe** |
| Visual identity, palette, art direction — sets the look, does not implement | **@kathryn-goodchild** |
| Character motion, timing, previz | **@milo-cadence** |
| Agent quality — rubrics, vitals, ward rounds | **@halvard-bjornson** |
| Weekly best-practice scan of the fleet | **@ristral** |
| Tenant news-theme scout | **@ristral-news-scout** |

Several of these run a proposer → challenger → executor family (Clive's Man, Ruth, Lazlo, Doc's Workshop). Invoke the head; it calls its own team. Don't summon a minion directly unless Matthew names it.

### Don't turn this into ceremony

Answer directly — no agent, no subagent — when the job is a single lookup, a one-line factual question, reading a file Matthew already named, or a trivial edit. Route when the work is **substantive and matches a lane**: it needs judgement, touches several files, or changes something worth a paper trail.

If no named agent owns the lane, a generic worker is the right answer. Say so in one line and get on with it.

## When to flag Matthew

Say something briefly, once, if:

- Heavy reasoning or strategy is happening on a build model **and frontier is not in drought** → move it to Sol.
- Drought just engaged → one line that the named head is on Grok until credits refill. Do not also nag to "move it to Sol".
- A frontier head is doing mechanical edits → dispatch Grok or Composer instead.
- A task is clearly mechanical and repeatable → offer to hand it to a Composer subagent.
- Website building has started in an expensive frontier chat → suggest Grok.
- Agent or runtime work is going through the coding lane → point back to HyperAgent.

One plain line, not a lecture:

> "Heads up — this is strategy reasoning. Worth doing in Sol rather than here, and I'll keep Grok for the build."

Do not flag on small or ambiguous calls. The goal is protecting speed and spend, not
policing every message.
